window.LLD_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Classes, Objects & References","f":"p1-classes-objects.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After Session a = new Session(\"u1\"); Session b = a; b.extend(30); what does a.ttlMinutes() return? (A new session starts at 30.)","o":["30, because only b was extended","60","It doesn't compile","It depends on when the garbage collector runs"],"a":1,"w":"b = a copies the reference, so a and b point at the same object. Extending it through b changes the one object both variables see, so it is now 60."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Classes, Objects & References","f":"p1-classes-objects.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"static void swap(Session x, Session y) { Session t = x; x = y; y = t; } is called as swap(a, b) , where a is user u1's session and b is u2's. What does a.userId() print afterwards?","o":["u2, because the sessions were swapped","It throws NullPointerException","u1, because only the method's copies of the references were swapped","u1, because swap made copies of both Session objects"],"a":2,"w":"Java copies each reference into x and y . The method swaps those two local copies, then its frame is discarded. The caller's a and b never changed. No object was copied either: only references are passed."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Classes, Objects & References","f":"p1-classes-objects.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Inside a method, Session s = new Session(\"u1\"); runs. Where do the variable s and the Session object live?","o":["s (a reference) is in the method's stack frame; the object is on the heap","Both are on the stack, because s is a local variable","Both are on the heap","s is on the heap; the object is in the stack frame"],"a":0,"w":"Local variables, including references, live in the frame of the call that declares them. Every object created with new lives on the heap. That is why the object can outlive the method, for example if it is returned or stored in a field."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Classes, Objects & References","f":"p1-classes-objects.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"When does a Session object become eligible for garbage collection?","o":["As soon as the method that created it returns","When its reference count drops to zero, so two objects pointing at each other are never collected","When you call System.gc()","When no chain of references from a GC root (a live frame, a static field) reaches it any more"],"a":3,"w":"Java's collectors use reachability from GC roots. An object returned or stored in a field survives its creating method. Cycles are collected once nothing outside reaches them. System.gc() is only a hint and never frees a reachable object."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Classes, Objects & References","f":"p1-classes-objects.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is private static int activeSessions , incremented and decremented from constructors and a close() method, a risky design?","o":["Static fields are stored on the stack, so the value is lost between calls","It is one global value shared by every test and every thread: tests leak state into each other, and concurrent updates can be lost","Static fields can't be changed after the class loads","Each Session object gets its own copy, so the count is always 1"],"a":1,"w":"A static field has exactly one copy for the whole program. That makes it global mutable state: hidden from constructors, shared across tests, and racy under concurrency unless guarded. A counter object passed to whoever needs it avoids all three problems."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Encapsulation & Invariants","f":"p2-encapsulation.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A field is declared as int tokens; with no modifier. Which code can read and write it?","o":["Only the class itself: no modifier means private","Any code anywhere: no modifier means public","The class itself and every class in the same package","The class itself and its subclasses only"],"a":2,"w":"Leaving out the modifier gives package-private access: the declaring class plus every class in the same package. To restrict it to the class, you must write private ."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Encapsulation & Invariants","f":"p2-encapsulation.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A TokenBucket has private fields but a public setTokens(int) with no validation, and callers write if (b.getTokens() > 0) b.setTokens(b.getTokens() - 1) . What is the core design problem?","o":["The class can't guarantee 0 ≤ tokens ≤ capacity: any caller can set any value, and the spending rule is copied into every caller","Getters are slower than reading a field directly","The fields should be public so callers don't need getters","Nothing: the fields are private, so it is encapsulated"],"a":0,"w":"Private fields with an unchecked setter give callers full control of the state, so the class can promise nothing. The fix is an operation like tryAcquire() that does the check and the change inside the class."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Encapsulation & Invariants","f":"p2-encapsulation.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An ApiKey constructor does this.scopes = scopes; . The code that created the key later runs scopes.add(\"admin:write\") on its own variable. What happens, and what is the fix?","o":["Nothing happens: the key has a private field, so the caller's change goes to a different set","It throws, because the key's field is final","The key gains the scope; fix it by making the field final","The key gains the scope; fix it by copying in the constructor, e.g. Set.copyOf(scopes)"],"a":3,"w":"The key stored a reference to the caller's set, so both share one object. final only stops the field being re-pointed; the set itself can still change. Copying in the constructor gives the key its own set that no one else can reach."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Encapsulation & Invariants","f":"p2-encapsulation.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A class returns Collections.unmodifiableList(orders) from a getter, and later adds a new order to orders internally. What does a caller holding the returned list see?","o":["The old contents: unmodifiableList made a copy","The new order too, because it is a read-only view; but the caller can't add or remove","An exception the next time it reads the list","The new order, and the caller can also add orders through it"],"a":1,"w":"unmodifiableList wraps the original list without copying. Reads go through to the live list, and writes throw UnsupportedOperationException . Use List.copyOf when you want a snapshot."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Encapsulation & Invariants","f":"p2-encapsulation.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is if (bucket.tryAcquire(now)) handle(req); a better design than if (bucket.available() > 0) { bucket.take(); handle(req); } ?","o":["Method calls with fewer characters run faster","It avoids needing a return value","The check and the change happen together inside the class, so the rule lives in one place and there's one method to make thread-safe","It lets callers skip the refill step when they are in a hurry"],"a":2,"w":"“Tell, don't ask.” Splitting the check ( available ) from the action ( take ) puts the rule in every caller and leaves a gap between check and act where another thread can take the last token. One method closes both problems."}];
